11	// Subscriptions are the in-memory, push-only counterpart of long polling.
12	//
13	// A subscription belongs to a (document, device) pair — the device is the one
14	// owning the session that opened the connection. It receives one coalesceable
15	// signal per change-producing commit (PostChanges, ReplayChanges,
16	// MergeChange, RestoreSnapshot all signal through the same hook long polling
17	// uses). A permission revoke targeting the pair is delivered as a separate,
18	// one-shot closed channel so the event cannot be missed or undone by a later
19	// grant on the same connection.
20	//
21	// Like parked long polls, subscriptions leave no durable trace: nothing is
22	// written to the database, a disconnect simply unregisters, and closing the
23	// store broadcasts one final wakeup (the server maps it to a going-away
24	// close). Pushing never allocates a cursor or inserts a change row; the
25	// subscription layer only observes commits.
